rpmsg: virtio: raise the buffer size to 2048

The X5H rpmsg-eth link carries Ethernet frames over RPMsg. With the
default 512-byte buffer the netif MTU is 462 and every DDS sample above
348 bytes fragments. 2048 fits a 1514-byte frame plus the 16-byte
RPMsg header. 512 buffers times 2048 is 1 MiB, which the demo device
tree reserves as the vdev0buffer carveout. The CR52 firmware sets the
matching RPMSG_BUFFER_SIZE.

That carveout is not optional, and an earlier version of this message
was wrong to say the pool came from cr52_ram1. Without a node named
vdev0buffer, rproc_add_virtio_dev() falls back to
of_reserved_mem_device_init_by_idx() on memory-region index 0, which
fails on a no-map node that carries no compatible. The pool then comes
from linux,cma@40000000, which no CR52 MPU region maps.

--- a/drivers/rpmsg/virtio_rpmsg_bus.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/virtio_rpmsg_bus.c
@@ -110,14 +110,14 @@
 	container_of(_rpdev, struct virtio_rpmsg_channel, rpdev)
 
 /*
- * We're allocating buffers of 512 bytes each for communications. The
+ * We're allocating buffers of 2048 bytes each for communications. The
  * number of buffers will be computed from the number of buffers supported
  * by the vring, upto a maximum of 512 buffers (256 in each direction).
  *
- * Each buffer will have 16 bytes for the msg header and 496 bytes for
+ * Each buffer will have 16 bytes for the msg header and 2032 bytes for
  * the payload.
  *
- * This will utilize a maximum total space of 256KB for the buffers.
+ * This will utilize a maximum total space of 1MB for the buffers.
  *
  * We might also want to add support for user-provided buffers in time.
  * This will allow bigger buffer size flexibility, and can also be used
@@ -128,7 +128,7 @@
  * processor.
  */
 #define MAX_RPMSG_NUM_BUFS	(512)
-#define MAX_RPMSG_BUF_SIZE	(512)
+#define MAX_RPMSG_BUF_SIZE	(2048)
 
